3 Selecting and opening
***********************

Click
     Selects the file: the one the next command acts on.

‘Ctrl’+click
     Adds the file to the selection, or takes it out.

‘Shift’+click
     Selects every file from the one selected last to the one
     clicked.

Double-click, or ‘RET’
     Opens it.  A folder opens in the same window.  A file that
     a window already has open goes to that window rather than
     opening a second time.  Text opens in Emacs, in one of its
     other frames.  Anything else opens in its usual program (as
     ‘~/.config/mimeapps.list’ says).

Right-click
     What can be done with the file under the mouse, or, on
     empty space, with the folder.

File, Open With
     Opens the selection with a program you name.

   Edit, Select All, Select None and Invert Selection work on
every file in the folder.  Dired's keys still mark: ‘m’ marks,
‘u’ unmarks, ‘U’ unmarks all.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Changing files,  Next: Drag and drop,  Prev: Selecting and opening,  Up: Top

4 Changing files
****************

Copy, Cut, Paste (‘M-w’, ‘C-w’, ‘C-y’)
     Copy or cut the selection, go to another folder, and paste.
     Pasting where the files already are makes copies beside
     them ("notes copy.txt").  Other file managers can paste
     what Esploro copied, and Esploro can paste what they
     copied.

Duplicate
     A copy of each selected file beside it.

Copy To, Move To
     Copies or moves the selection into a folder you type.

Rename (‘F2’)
     A new name for the file selected.

New Folder (‘+’)
     A folder in the one you're in.

Move to Trash (‘Delete’)
     Puts the selection in the Trash.  Nothing is deleted for
     good there.

Undo (‘C-/’)
     Puts back the last change Esploro made.  Undo again for the
     one before.  Undoing a copy puts the copy in the Trash,
     rather than deleting it.

   Every change is a plan, a short list of steps, checked whole
before anything happens: a file that would be overwritten, or a
folder that isn't there, stops it with nothing changed, and the
message says why.  Changes run in the background, so a long copy
doesn't stop Emacs.  Each one is kept in a journal
(‘~/.local/state/esploro/journal/’), which is what undo reads.

   Dired's own keys (‘C’, ‘R’, ‘D’ and the rest) still work in
the list, but around the journal: what they do can't be undone
from Esploro.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Drag and drop,  Next: The preview,  Prev: Changing files,  Up: Top

5 Drag and drop
***************

In
     Drag files from another program onto the list: they're
     copied into the folder (moved, when the other program says
     so, often with ‘Shift’).  Like every change, it can be
     undone.  Files dropped on the folder they're already in are
     left alone.

Out
     Press on a file anywhere on its row and drag it to another
     program: a browser's upload, a mail, a terminal (which
     types its name).  It's copied; hold ‘Shift’ to move it.
     Selected files go together.

Between panes
     Drag from one pane to the other: the file is copied into
     that pane's folder.


File: esploro.info,  Node: The preview,  Next: Two panes,  Prev: Drag and drop,  Up: Top

6 The preview
*************

Down the right of the window, the file under the cursor:

A picture
     The picture, scaled to fit.
A PDF, a video
     The PDF's first page, or a frame of the video.  Esploro
     makes it once, in the background ("making a preview..."
     while it does), and keeps it in ‘~/.cache/esploro’, so the
     next look is at once.
Text, code
     Its first lines, in the colours its mode gives them.
A folder
     How many things are in it, and their names.
Anything else
     What it is, as ‘file’ says.
Several selected
     How many, their size together, and their names.

   Below each: its size, when it last changed, and the windows
that have it open.  The preview follows the cursor and the mouse
once they rest, so holding an arrow key down stays quick.  With
two panes, it shows the pane used last; each Esploro window has
its own.

   ‘F11’ (View, Preview, or the tool bar) hides it, or shows it
again.  Hidden stays hidden, in every Esploro window, until you
turn it on.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Two panes,  Next: Windows and workspaces,  Prev: The preview,  Up: Top

7 Two panes
***********

‘F3’ (View, Two Panes, or the tool bar) shows two folders side
by side.  The new pane starts at the same folder; each then has
its own folder, history, sort and hidden files.  ‘F3’ again goes
back to one.

Copy to Other Pane (‘C-c C-c’)
Move to Other Pane (‘F6’)
     The selection, into the other pane's folder.  Both are on
     the Edit menu and the right-click menu.

   The places and the tool bar act on the pane used last.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Windows and workspaces,  Next: Looking around,  Prev: Two panes,  Up: Top

8 Windows and workspaces
************************

Each workspace can have its own Esploro window, at its own
folder.  ‘Super+e’ goes to the one on the workspace you're on,
or opens one there; one on another workspace stays where it is.
To have two on the same workspace, use ‘Super+Alt+Shift+e’,
File, New Window (‘C-x 5 2’), or ‘esploro --new’.

   Esploro knows which window is on which workspace by asking
StumpWM. Without StumpWM, ‘esploro FOLDER’ uses any Esploro
window.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Looking around,  Next: The Trash,  Prev: Windows and workspaces,  Up: Top

9 Looking around
****************

Sorting (View, or ‘s’)
     By name, size (largest first), time (newest first) or kind.
     The same again, or View, The Other Way Round, turns it
     round.  ‘s’ goes through them in turn.

Hidden files (‘.’)
     Shows or hides the files whose names start with a dot.

Filter (‘/’)
     Shows only the names holding some text, in any case, until
     you refresh (‘F5’).

Find Below (‘M-s f’)
     Every file below this folder whose name matches, as a list
     you can open, copy and drag from like any folder.  Back
     returns.

Going (‘M-Left’, ‘M-Right’, ‘M-Up’, ‘C-l’)
     Back, forward, the folder above, and a folder typed, with
     completion.


File: esploro.info,  Node: The Trash,  Next: Where files are open,  Prev: Looking around,  Up: Top

10 The Trash
************

Esploro uses the Trash other programs use (the freedesktop one,
in ‘~/.local/share/Trash’).  Go, The Trash (or Trash in the
places) shows what's in it.  There, Restore from the Trash puts
the selection back where it was; undo puts it in the Trash
again.  Empty the Trash deletes everything in it, for good,
after asking: that one can't be undone.


File: esploro.info,  Node: Where files are open,  Next: Keys and mouse,  Prev: The Trash,  Up: Top

11 Where files are open
***********************

Beside a file that a window has open, Esploro says which: "open
in Emacs on 2 (unsaved)", "open in mpv on 4".  It asks StumpWM
for the windows and their programs, ‘/proc’ for what those hold
open or were started on, and Emacs for its buffers.  Opening
such a file goes to its window instead of opening it again.


